---
import ContentLayout from '@/layouts/ContentLayout.astro';
import Card from '@/components/ui/Card.astro';

const breadcrumbs = [{ name: 'Guides', url: '/guides/' }];
---

<ContentLayout
  title="LIC Policy Guides & Educational Resources"
  description="Comprehensive guides on LIC policy management, surrender calculations, bonus mechanisms, and tax benefits."
  pageTitle="Policy Guides & Articles"
  pageSubtitle="Practical, unbiased explanations of insurance concepts and policy lifecycle management."
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
>
  <div class="space-y-6">
    <Card title="New: LIC Surrender Rules Changed (IRDAI, 1 Oct 2024) — Value After 1 Year">
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mb-3">
        Surrendering in the first year used to pay nothing. IRDAI's revised norms now make a fairer, G-Sec-linked surrender value payable after the first policy year. See the old rule vs new rule with a worked example.
      </p>
      <a href="/guides/lic-irdai-surrender-rules-2024/" class="text-xs font-semibold text-brand-700 hover:text-brand-800">
        Read the New Rules Guide &rarr;
      </a>
    </Card>

    <Card title="New: LIC Surrender After 5 Years — How Much You Get, How Much You Lose">
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mb-3">
        Paid premiums for 5 years and thinking of exiting? An illustrative calculation (₹30,000/yr for 5 years) shows the payout, the ~45% immediate loss, and why pricing the paid-up option first can save money.
      </p>
      <a href="/guides/lic-surrender-after-5-years/" class="text-xs font-semibold text-brand-700 hover:text-brand-800">
        Read the Year-5 Surrender Guide &rarr;
      </a>
    </Card>

    <Card title="New: How to Check LIC Policy Status — Online, SMS & WhatsApp (5 Methods)">
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mb-3">
        Is your policy in-force, lapsed, paid-up or matured? Five working ways to check in minutes — Customer Portal, WhatsApp, ASKLIC SMS, helpline and branch — plus what each status means and your next step.
      </p>
      <a href="/guides/lic-policy-status-check/" class="text-xs font-semibold text-brand-700 hover:text-brand-800">
        Check Your Policy Status &rarr;
      </a>
    </Card>

    <Card title="New: LIC Policy Lapsed? Revival Process, Cost & Last Date (5-Year Rule)">
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mb-3">
        Cover stopped after a missed premium? Most traditional policies can be revived within 5 years of the first unpaid premium. See the arrears-plus-interest cost in a worked example, the health-declaration step, and when revival beats going paid-up.
      </p>
      <a href="/guides/lic-policy-revival/" class="text-xs font-semibold text-brand-700 hover:text-brand-800">
        Read the Revival Guide &rarr;
      </a>
    </Card>

    <Card title="New: Missed Your LIC Premium Date? Grace Period, Late Fee & What Happens Next">
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mb-3">
        You have 30 grace days to pay late (only 15 for monthly premiums), cover continues during grace, and interest applies only once arrears go into revival. A worked example shows the ₹0 vs ~₹8,800 difference.
      </p>
      <a href="/guides/lic-premium-grace-period-late-fee/" class="text-xs font-semibold text-brand-700 hover:text-brand-800">
        Read the Grace Period Guide &rarr;
      </a>
    </Card>

    <Card title="New: LIC Premium Shows No GST? The 0% GST Change Explained">
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mb-3">
        Since 22 Sep 2025 individual life insurance is GST-exempt. See your actual saving — 2.25% on endowment renewals, 18% on term — and how to check your next premium notice or receipt.
      </p>
      <a href="/guides/lic-gst-0-percent-premium-change/" class="text-xs font-semibold text-brand-700 hover:text-brand-800">
        Read the 0% GST Guide &rarr;
      </a>
    </Card>

    <Card title="New: LIC Policy Maturing? Maturity Claim with Form 3825 — Documents & Steps">
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mb-3">
        Form 3825 is the discharge receipt at the centre of every maturity claim. What to submit (original bond, NEFT mandate, ID proof), the 1-month submission rule, and the deductions to expect.
      </p>
      <a href="/guides/lic-maturity-claim-form-3825/" class="text-xs font-semibold text-brand-700 hover:text-brand-800">
        Read the Maturity Claim Guide &rarr;
      </a>
    </Card>

    <Card title="New: LIC Death Claim — Complete Documents Checklist for the Nominee">
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mb-3">
        The exact sequence: intimation letter, Claim Form 3783, discharge Form 3801, death certificate, original policy bond and NEFT — plus the extra forms LIC asks when death is within the first 3 years.
      </p>
      <a href="/guides/lic-death-claim-documents-nominee/" class="text-xs font-semibold text-brand-700 hover:text-brand-800">
        Read the Death Claim Checklist &rarr;
      </a>
    </Card>

    <Card title="New: Is LIC Maturity Tax-Free? Section 10(10D) & the ₹5 Lakh Rule">
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mb-3">
        Most LIC maturities are tax-free — but two tests decide yours: premium ≤10% of sum assured, and for policies issued on/after 1 Apr 2023, aggregate premiums within ₹5 lakh a year. Death claims stay exempt, always.
      </p>
      <a href="/guides/lic-maturity-tax-10-10d-explained/" class="text-xs font-semibold text-brand-700 hover:text-brand-800">
        Read the Maturity Tax Guide &rarr;
      </a>
    </Card>

    <Card title="New: LIC Policy Loan Interest Rate — Real Cost Over 1, 3 & 5 Years">
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mb-3">
        No single published rate exists; plan documents most often cite ~10.5% p.a. compounded half-yearly. See what a ₹1 lakh loan really costs at 1, 3 and 5 years, and the interest-only habit to avoid.
      </p>
      <a href="/guides/lic-policy-loan-interest-rate/" class="text-xs font-semibold text-brand-700 hover:text-brand-800">
        Read the Loan Interest Guide &rarr;
      </a>
    </Card>

    <Card title="New: How to Change the Nominee in Your LIC Policy (Form 3750)">
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mb-3">
        A nomination counts only when LIC registers it (Section 39). The branch process with Form 3750, multiple nominees with percentage shares, the minor-nominee appointee, and the acknowledgement to keep.
      </p>
      <a href="/guides/lic-change-nominee-form-3750/" class="text-xs font-semibold text-brand-700 hover:text-brand-800">
        Read the Nominee Change Guide &rarr;
      </a>
    </Card>

    <Card title="New: LIC Jeevan Labh (736) Returns — ₹10 Lakh SA Example">
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mb-3">
        Limited pay 10/15/16 years, covered for 16/21/25 with bonuses accruing through the premium-free tail. How the maturity figure is built, an illustrative ₹10 lakh calculation (~5.8% zone), and who the plan suits.
      </p>
      <a href="/guides/lic-jeevan-labh-736-returns/" class="text-xs font-semibold text-brand-700 hover:text-brand-800">
        Read the Jeevan Labh Guide &rarr;
      </a>
    </Card>

    <Card title="New: LIC Jeevan Umang (745) — The “8% Yearly Income” Reality Check">
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mb-3">
        The 8% is of the sum assured, not a return on your premiums. Pay 15–30 years, then ₹80,000/yr per ₹10L SA till age 100, plus SA + bonuses — who that structure suits, and who it doesn't.
      </p>
      <a href="/guides/lic-jeevan-umang-745-income-reality/" class="text-xs font-semibold text-brand-700 hover:text-brand-800">
        Read the Jeevan Umang Guide &rarr;
      </a>
    </Card>

    <Card title="New: LIC Jeevan Utsav (771) — Why “10% Income” Is Not a 10% Return">
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mb-3">
        The 10% is of the sum assured, income starts years after premiums end, and there is no bonus — only ₹40/1000 guaranteed additions. Regular vs Flexi (5.5%) choice explained.
      </p>
      <a href="/guides/lic-jeevan-utsav-771-income-truth/" class="text-xs font-semibold text-brand-700 hover:text-brand-800">
        Read the Jeevan Utsav Guide &rarr;
      </a>
    </Card>

    <Card title="New: Surrendering LIC After 10 Years — Value, Loss & Paid-Up Option">
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mb-3">
        At year 10 the cash loss is usually small (≈₹4.6–5.1 lakh back on ₹5 lakh paid in the example); the real cost is the bonuses and cover you abandon. Why paid-up often wins now, plus the Form 5074 process.
      </p>
      <a href="/guides/lic-surrender-after-10-years/" class="text-xs font-semibold text-brand-700 hover:text-brand-800">
        Read the Year-10 Surrender Guide &rarr;
      </a>
    </Card>

    <Card title="New: LIC Premium Payment Online — Pay Direct, UPI Apps & Receipt">
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mb-3">
        Pay without a login via Pay Direct, through the Customer Portal, or from any UPI app — plus receipt download afterwards, the credit-card fee to avoid, and the 6–8 day double-payment refund.
      </p>
      <a href="/guides/lic-premium-payment-online-receipt/" class="text-xs font-semibold text-brand-700 hover:text-brand-800">
        Read the Online Payment Guide &rarr;
      </a>
    </Card>

    <Card title="New: How Much Loan Can You Get on Your LIC Policy? (90% Rule)">
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mb-3">
        Up to 90% of surrender value if in force, 85% if paid-up — ₹7.5L surrender value means a ₹6.75L loan. Eligibility conditions, half-yearly interest, and why no credit score is needed.
      </p>
      <a href="/guides/lic-how-much-loan-on-policy/" class="text-xs font-semibold text-brand-700 hover:text-brand-800">
        Read the Loan Amount Guide &rarr;
      </a>
    </Card>

    <Card title="New: LIC Jeevan Shanti (758) — Pension on ₹10 Lakh, Single vs Joint Life">
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mb-3">
        Invest once, pension for life after a 1–12 year deferment, at rates locked today. ~₹99,400/yr single vs ~₹94,100 joint on ₹10 lakh at 45, the monthly-mode −4% haircut, and who wins each choice.
      </p>
      <a href="/guides/lic-jeevan-shanti-758-pension/" class="text-xs font-semibold text-brand-700 hover:text-brand-800">
        Read the Jeevan Shanti Guide &rarr;
      </a>
    </Card>

    <Card title="New: Lost Your LIC Policy Bond? Duplicate Bond via Form 3756">
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mb-3">
        Losing the bond doesn't cancel the policy. The Form 3756 indemnity route, Form 3807 surety above ₹50,000, when a newspaper ad is required, and the claim-time shortcut if maturity is near.
      </p>
      <a href="/guides/lic-duplicate-policy-bond-form-3756/" class="text-xs font-semibold text-brand-700 hover:text-brand-800">
        Read the Duplicate Bond Guide &rarr;
      </a>
    </Card>

    <Card title="New: LIC Money-Back vs Endowment — Which Pays You Better?">
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mb-3">
        Same ₹10 lakh sum assured, very different cash flow: money-back pays 20% at years 5/10/15 plus 40% at maturity, endowment pays everything at the end. The death-benefit twist, the premium gap, and the reinvestment question.
      </p>
      <a href="/guides/lic-money-back-vs-endowment/" class="text-xs font-semibold text-brand-700 hover:text-brand-800">
        Read the Comparison Guide &rarr;
      </a>
    </Card>

    <Card title="New: LIC Child Plans — Amritbaal (774) vs Children's Money Back">
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mb-3">
        Amritbaal guarantees ₹80 per ₹1,000 per year with maturity at an age you pick (18–25); Children's Money Back stages 20% payouts at 18, 20, 22. Plus the premium-waiver rider that actually justifies either plan.
      </p>
      <a href="/guides/lic-child-plan-amritbaal-774/" class="text-xs font-semibold text-brand-700 hover:text-brand-800">
        Read the Child Plans Guide &rarr;
      </a>
    </Card>

    <Card title="New: How Much Term Insurance Do You Actually Need?">
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mb-3">
        Forget the 10× thumb rule. The expense method: family expenses × support years + loans + goals − assets — with a worked ₹8-lakh-income example showing why it lands near ₹1.4 crore, and why cover should end at 60–65.
      </p>
      <a href="/guides/lic-term-cover-how-much/" class="text-xs font-semibold text-brand-700 hover:text-brand-800">
        Read the Term Cover Guide &rarr;
      </a>
    </Card>

    <Card title="New: LIC Single Premium Plans — Pay Once, Stay Covered">
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mb-3">
        One lump sum, no renewals: Single Premium Endowment pays SA + bonuses at maturity, Bima Bachat stages payouts over 9–15 years. Surrender values of 70%/90%, plus the 10(10D) tax test a big single premium must pass first.
      </p>
      <a href="/guides/lic-single-premium-plans/" class="text-xs font-semibold text-brand-700 hover:text-brand-800">
        Read the Single Premium Guide &rarr;
      </a>
    </Card>

    <Card title="New: LIC Survival Benefit Claim — Your Money-Back Instalment Is Due">
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mb-3">
        Premiums must be paid to the due date, then discharge receipt + NEFT. Missed an instalment years ago? It sits as an unclaimed amount — and can even offset revival dues in money-back policies.
      </p>
      <a href="/guides/lic-survival-benefit-claim/" class="text-xs font-semibold text-brand-700 hover:text-brand-800">
        Read the Survival Benefit Guide &rarr;
      </a>
    </Card>

    <Card title="New: LIC Bonus Rate — How to Check What Your Plan Was Declared">
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mb-3">
        The bonus rate is per ₹1,000 of sum assured after LIC's 31-March valuation, and differs by plan and term band. Find the official plan-wise chart, turn ₹44/1,000 into rupees on a ₹5 lakh policy, and learn why non-par plans show no bonus.
      </p>
      <a href="/guides/lic-bonus-rate-check-plan/" class="text-xs font-semibold text-brand-700 hover:text-brand-800">
        Read the Bonus Rate Guide &rarr;
      </a>
    </Card>

    <Card title="New: How to Check Your LIC Surrender Value — Portal, Phone, Branch">
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mb-3">
        Portal enrolment shows surrender value under Loan & Bonus; the 022-6827-6827 IVR loan menu proxies it; the branch's written quotation is the only number LIC pays. Form 5074 steps if you proceed.
      </p>
      <a href="/guides/lic-check-surrender-value/" class="text-xs font-semibold text-brand-700 hover:text-brand-800">
        Read the Surrender Value Check Guide &rarr;
      </a>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Guide: What Happens When You Surrender an LIC Policy?">
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mb-3">
        Surrendering an insurance policy terminates life coverage and converts the policy into a cash payout. Learn how Guaranteed Surrender Value (GSV) and Special Surrender Value (SSV) are determined.
      </p>
      <a href="/lic-surrender-value-calculator/" class="text-xs font-semibold text-brand-700 hover:text-brand-800">
        Read Guide & Calculate Surrender &rarr;
      </a>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Guide: Paid-Up Policy vs Surrender — Which is Better?">
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mb-3">
        If you are unable to pay further premiums, making your policy "Paid-Up" allows you to retain proportional death and maturity benefits without losing a massive chunk of your invested premiums.
      </p>
      <a href="/lic-surrender-loss-calculator/" class="text-xs font-semibold text-brand-700 hover:text-brand-800">
        Compare Paid-Up vs Surrender &rarr;
      </a>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Guide: How LIC Bonus Rates Work">
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mb-3">
        Learn how Simple Reversionary Bonuses and Final Additional Bonuses (FAB) are declared annually by LIC and how they compound over a 20+ year term.
      </p>
      <a href="/lic-bonus-calculator/" class="text-xs font-semibold text-brand-700 hover:text-brand-800">
        Bonus Calculation Guide &rarr;
      </a>
    </Card>
  </div>
</ContentLayout>
